      "writtenOn": "2021-03-10T20:35:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I still don\u0027t see the benefit of the node filter here. It seems like if process_num_buffers \u003e 50 and a quarter of buffer space is consumed, then we\u0027ve found our culprit.\n\nYou are concerned about a potentially useless stack if this happens to be another thread of the same process accessing a different node? Do you really think that is a significant risk? I think it would be extremely rare (if ever). If there are \u003e50 spammy txns, the probability that the same process is sending interleaved legitimate async txns to the same target proc is very low.\n\nI\u0027d prefer to simplify and drop the node filter.",
      "revId": "333762bd02db7f6a5426a7efcb0ae9f1c6583f24",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2df729ed_c2d8a536",
        "filename": "drivers/android/binder_alloc.c",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 391,
      "author": {
        "id": 1862735
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-11T06:58:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "OK, agree that the probability is very low. So let me drop the node filter.\n\nBelow is what Martijn said previous:\nOne other consideration is that the current transaction is not necessarily the method call that is responsible for overflowing the transaction buffer, though there is a decent chance that it is. One thing we could do is count the size of the outstanding transactions to the node that the current caller is targeting, and if indeed the majority also goes to this node, that is strong evidence that it is calls into this node that are responsible for overflowing the buffer. Then, together with the backtrace, that gives us interface + method name of the transaction, which should make this even easier to debug when it happens.\n\nAnyway, seems node filter take much cost but little effect(the situation probability is very low). Though it can deliever 5 BR_SPAM_SUSPECT to user-space if a bad client sends spam(10 more times) to each of 5 nodes. \n\nLet\u0027s deal with the main situation and simplify the design.",
      "parentUuid": "0b361748_a25b2310",
      "revId": "333762bd02db7f6a5426a7efcb0ae9f1c6583f24",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
